    7 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Verification in Remote Areas',
                'summary' => 'Collect defensible evidence far from the network.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Some of the most important verifications happen far from roads, power, and signal, and the agent who prepares for remote work brings back evidence that survives review while the unprepared agent brings back nothing.\n\nStart with the device. Charge fully, clear storage for photos, and download any offline maps or visit lists before leaving coverage. A phone that dies at the farm is a visit with no evidence, and a second trip costs everyone a day. Carry a power bank, and keep the device warm in cold weather, because cold batteries fail at the moment they are needed most.\n\nPlan the evidence sequence before you arrive. In poor signal areas, capture GPS and photos first, then submit records when you return to coverage, rather than attempting a live upload at the farm. Know the app's offline behaviour: what it saves locally, what it timestamps, and what it requires a connection for. The agent who knows the flow captures the evidence once and submits it once.\n\nHandle the geography honestly. Remote farms often lack clear entrances, so capture the coordinate at the farm's identifiable point, with a landmark photo that ties the coordinate to the place, and note the access route in your report. For farms reached only by footpath, record the last reachable point plus the landmark, and describe the walk in the notes.\n\nFinally, plan the return trip: fuel, time, and the safety check-in. Remote verification is fieldwork, and the agent who plans the route, the evidence, and the return is the agent whose remote verifications pass review without a follow-up visit.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Handling Difficult Visits and Conflicts',
                'summary' => 'Stay professional when the visit turns.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Most verifications are routine, but some visits turn difficult: an absent grower, a farm that does not match the profile, an angry visitor, or pressure to approve. The agent who handles conflict professionally protects the program and themselves.\n\nStart with preparation, because most difficult visits are surprised ones. Review the profile and the visit objective before you travel, and know what you will do if the farm does not match, the grower is absent, or the visit is refused. The agent with a plan for the difficult case is the agent who stays calm when the case arrives.\n\nWhen the farm does not match the profile, document, do not accuse. Record exactly what you found, the discrepancy, the evidence, and any explanation offered, and let the verification team decide. A calm, factual record is the evidence the team needs; an accusation is a conflict the team must survive. You verify facts; you do not judge people.\n\nWhen a grower is hostile or refuses the visit, do not argue and do not force the issue. Record the refusal, the time, and the circumstances, and report it. A refused visit is a finding, and the program decides what it means. Leaving safely is always the correct outcome of a hostile visit.\n\nFinally, handle pressure with the same calm. Anyone who urges you to approve quickly, offers a payment, or claims a special status is testing your integrity. Politely follow the process, record the pressure attempt with the person's name and context, and report it immediately. The agent who stays calm, documents everything, and reports pressure is the agent the program trusts with its most valuable work.",
            ],
        ],
