    8 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Economic Thresholds and Cost-Benefit Advice',
                'summary' => 'Only treat when the math says treat.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Every recommendation costs the farmer money, and the agronomist who advises on economics, not just on biology, protects the farmer's profit as well as their crop. The economic threshold is the pest or damage level at which treatment pays for itself.\n\nStart with the threshold concept: a treatment is justified only when the cost of the treatment is less than the loss it prevents. A few caterpillars on a mature palm rarely justify a spray; the same infestation on a young nursery batch might. The advisor who treats every sighting treats the advisor's anxiety, not the farmer's field.\n\nEstimate the loss honestly. What is the damage worth in nuts, in quality, or in stand loss, and what will the treatment cost including labour, product, and equipment? The comparison needs numbers, and the numbers need to be the farmer's numbers: their price, their cost, their block. A treatment that pays on one farm may lose money on another.\n\nPresent the options with the economics attached: the ideal correction, the lower-cost interim measure, and the do-nothing baseline with its expected loss. The farmer should choose with the costs visible, because a treatment the farmer cannot afford is a recommendation that fails, and a do-nothing that costs less than the cure is sometimes the correct advice.\n\nFinally, record the reasoning in the case: the threshold, the estimate, the options, and the farmer's choice. The documented economic reasoning is what makes your advice reviewable, what teaches the next advisor, and what proves the advice was sound when the harvest answers it.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Advisory Tools: Kits, Tests, and References',
                "summary" => "Carry the tools that make diagnosis cheaper.",
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Field diagnosis is cheaper with the right tools, and the agronomist who carries a small kit and knows the references gets the answer in the field instead of after a laboratory wait. The tools do not replace expertise; they make expertise faster.\n\nStart with the field kit: a hand lens for pests and disease signs, a soil test kit where available, a tape measure, a sample bag, and a notebook. The lens that finds the mite or the scale insect in the field is the difference between a same-day diagnosis and a wrong guess, and the sample bag preserves the evidence for a lab referral.\n\nKnow the references cold: the pest and disease identification cards, the nutrient sign chart, and the treatment plan builder. The advisor who can identify the rhinoceros beetle's bore holes and V-shaped crown cuts without the card is the advisor who earns trust in the field, and the card that confirms the identification earns it faster.\n\nUse laboratory and specialist referral deliberately. When the pattern does not match a known pest, disease, or deficiency, refer rather than spray broadly. A broad spray kills beneficial insects, wastes money, and can leave residues, while a referral costs a day and names the real culprit. Document the referral with photos and the case history.\n\nFinally, keep the tools current. New pests arrive, labels change, and references age. The advisor who updates their kit and references is the advisor whose diagnosis stays accurate, and accurate diagnosis is the cheapest, safest, and most effective control the farmer can buy.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    9 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Product Recalls and Safety Response',
                'summary' => 'Act fast when a product becomes unsafe.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "When a product is found to be unsafe, mislabelled, or counterfeit, the provider who responds fast and openly protects their customers and their accreditation, while the provider who hides the problem loses both. Recall readiness is a compliance discipline, not a crisis improvisation.\n\nStart with the traceability that makes a recall possible. Your stock records with batch and expiry, your supplier invoices, and your sale records are what let you answer the question 'which customers received this batch, and when'. A provider who can answer in minutes runs a contained recall; one who cannot answer at all runs a scandal.\n\nNext, know the escalation path. When a safety concern surfaces, stop selling the batch immediately, quarantine the remaining stock, and notify the platform and the relevant authority as the product's registration requires. Speed matters: every hour a recalled product stays on the shelf is an hour a grower can be harmed by it.\n\nCommunicate honestly. Notify affected buyers through the platform with the batch, the issue, and the remedy: return, refund, or replacement. Honest, fast communication is the difference between a provider who managed a recall and a provider who caused a scandal, and the platform's dispute records are the channel that keeps the communication clean.\n\nFinally, run the post-recall review: what failed, where, and why, and fix the process that allowed it. The provider who treats a recall as a system failure, not a bad luck event, is the provider whose next decade is quiet, and quiet compliance is the accreditation's real test.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Supplier Qualification and Audits',
                'summary' => 'Verify the source before you stock it.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Your accreditation covers what you sell, and what you sell starts with who you buy from. Supplier qualification is the discipline of verifying the source before the product reaches your shelf, and it is the first gate of your quality system.\n\nStart with the supplier's credentials: business registration, product registrations for the categories they sell, and their own storage and handling standards. A supplier who cannot show registration evidence for a regulated product is a supplier whose product should not be on your shelf, however good the margin looks.\n\nNext, verify the product paperwork per shipment: labels, registration, batch numbers, and expiry. Check that the paperwork matches the physical product, because repackaged or mislabelled products are the classic counterfeit route. The intake check that takes two minutes per shipment is the check that prevents a two-year accreditation loss.\n\nAudit your suppliers on a schedule. Visit the supplier's depot, check their storage and stock rotation, and review their documentation discipline. The supplier audit is the evidence that your supply chain is legitimate, and it is the evidence an inspector asks for when your own accreditation is sample-checked.\n\nFinally, keep a supplier file: credentials, audits, invoices, and any incidents. The file is your defence in a recall, your leverage in a dispute, and your proof of due diligence. The provider who qualifies and audits their suppliers is the provider whose accreditation survives every inspection.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    10 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Managing Seasonal Demand Surges',
                'summary' => 'Scale up without breaking quality.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Demand surges are the service business's best opportunity and its most common failure. The provider who scales for the peak keeps quality and earns the season; the one who scales blindly breaks the standard and loses the reputation the surge was meant to build.\n\nStart with the forecast. Last year's bookings, this year's inquiries, and the season's calendar tell you when the surge comes and how big it is. The provider who books the surge in advance, with staff and equipment reserved, serves the peak; the provider who discovers it on the day turns it away.\n\nNext, build the capacity deliberately: trained staff, maintained equipment, and staged materials. Add capacity only when the standard is documented and trainable, because a surge staffed by untrained workers is a surge delivered at a lower standard. The written method is what lets you add people without adding defects.\n\nManage the queue honestly. When demand exceeds capacity, communicate lead times clearly rather than overbooking. A booked job delivered late is a complaint; an honest two-week lead time is a booking kept. The provider who protects the standard during the surge keeps the reviews that fill the next one.\n\nFinally, debrief after the peak: what was booked, what slipped, and what the delays cost. The pattern in the debrief is next season's plan. The provider who manages surges with a forecast, a standard, and an honest queue grows the business the surge rewards.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Service Recovery and Complaint Handling',
                'summary' => 'Fix the failure and keep the customer.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Every service fails sometimes, and the failure is not what costs the customer; the mishandled failure is. Service recovery is the discipline of fixing the failure so well that the customer stays more loyal than if nothing had gone wrong.\n\nStart with the acknowledgement. When a customer complains, acknowledge the failure in your own words before explaining or defending. The customer who feels heard is already halfway back, and the provider who acknowledges first resolves fastest. Never argue with the customer's experience; it is their experience, and it is the data.\n\nNext, fix what can be fixed: redo the job, refund through the platform, or discount the next service. Offer the remedy clearly and deliver it through the platform where the record is clean. A remedy delivered through the platform is a remedy both sides can see, and a clear remedy closes the complaint.\n\nThen find the root cause. Was it the method, the equipment, the staff, or the scheduling? The complaint that is fixed once is a recovery; the complaint that repeats is a business problem. Fix the cause, train the team, and note it in your improvement log, because the cause fixed once is the complaint that never arrives again.\n\nFinally, follow up after the remedy, and ask whether the customer is satisfied. The provider who handles the failure well is the provider whose reviews stay strong, because every service business fails occasionally; only some businesses recover.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    11 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Packing and Presentation Standards',
                'summary' => 'Pack so the product arrives as promised.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Packing is where the listing's promise becomes the buyer's experience, and the seller whose packing is standardised delivers a product that arrives as promised while the seller who packs ad hoc delivers disputes. The packing standard is a short checklist applied to every order.\n\nStart with the container. Use clean, dry containers suited to the product: breathable sacks for fresh nuts, sturdy crates for bulk, and closed packaging against rain and pests. A container that has held chemicals or that is damp contaminates the produce it is meant to protect, and the contamination is a refund the seller pays for twice.\n\nNext, protect against movement. Fill the container properly, cushion the top layer, and avoid overfilling, which crushes the lower layers. For fresh or de-husked produce, keep it off the ground and out of direct sun during packing, and never pack wet produce; moisture is the enemy that turns a good dispatch into mould at delivery.\n\nLabel and document every consignment: the grade, the count or weight, and the order reference on the container, plus a photo of the packed consignment before handover. The photo is the evidence that settles any dispute about what was dispatched, and the label is what keeps the order and the delivery matched at the receiving end.\n\nFinally, hand over cleanly: confirm the quantity and grade with the driver or buyer at handover, and keep the reference. The seller who packs to a standard delivers a product that arrives as it left, and that is the seller whose buyers return every season.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Fulfilment at Scale',
                'summary' => 'Deliver fifty orders as well as five.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Five orders are a hobby; fifty are a business, and the difference is a fulfilment process that does not depend on the seller's memory. The seller who builds fulfilment at scale grows without breaking quality, and the one who does not discovers the limit the hard way.\n\nStart with the order pipeline: a single place where every order lives, with its product, grade, quantity, price, and delivery terms. The platform order record is that place, and the seller who works only from the platform never loses an order to a phone message or a notebook page.\n\nNext, batch the work. Pick, pack, and dispatch in batches by area or by date, so the travel is planned and the packing is consistent. The batched seller dispatches more orders in less time, with fewer errors, than the one who processes each order in a scramble, and the schedule is what makes the batch possible.\n\nStage the logistics: the vehicle, the containers, and the route planned the evening before, with the delivery list and the references. The seller who stages the night before is the seller who delivers on time, and on-time delivery is the metric that decides your marketplace ranking.\n\nFinally, measure the fulfilment: on-time rate, damage rate, and shortfall rate, reviewed weekly. The numbers show whether the process or the capacity is the constraint, and the seller who reads the numbers fixes the constraint before the customer feels it. Fulfilment at scale is fulfilment on schedule.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    12 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Field Force Performance Reviews',
                'summary' => 'Review agents on evidence, not impressions.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The state's coverage depends on the field force, and the coordinator who reviews agents on evidence, not impressions, keeps the team honest and improving. A performance review is a short, structured look at the agent's numbers and their evidence.\n\nStart with the activity numbers: visits completed, verifications submitted, and same-day submission rate. The agent who completes the visit list and submits the same day is the agent whose work the verification team can trust, and the numbers show it without argument.\n\nNext, review the evidence quality. Sample a few of the agent's visit records: the GPS fix quality, the photo set, the factual notes, and the completeness of the declared facts. Evidence quality is the agent's real product, and the sample review is the cheapest quality control the state has.\n\nThen review the outcomes: verification pass rate, escalation quality, and the follow-ups the agent handled. A high pass rate with clean evidence is the goal; a high pass rate with thin evidence is a red flag, and a low pass rate with clean evidence is a training need. The numbers tell you which.\n\nFinally, hold the review as a conversation with the evidence in front of both of you. Agree on one improvement for the next period, and check it at the next review. The coordinator who reviews on evidence builds a field force that improves quarter by quarter, and the improving field force is the state's coverage.",
