                'title' => 'Handling Customer Complaints and Recalls',
                'summary' => 'Own the problem and fix the cause.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Complaints and recalls are the provider's most visible moments, and the provider who owns the problem keeps the accreditation while the one who hides it loses the badge. The discipline is the same for a single complaint and a full recall: acknowledge, fix, find the cause, and communicate.\n\nStart with the acknowledgement. Every complaint deserves a same-day response that acknowledges the problem in the customer's own terms. The customer who is acknowledged is a customer who can be kept, and the complaint acknowledged in the platform's message history is the complaint with a clean record for both sides.\n\nNext, fix what can be fixed through the platform: replacement, refund, or a corrected service. The remedy delivered through the platform is the remedy both sides can see and the dispute team can trace. A remedy delivered off-platform is a remedy nobody can prove, and it removes the protection the platform exists to provide.\n\nFor a recall, act faster: stop selling the batch, quarantine the stock, notify the platform and the authority as the registration requires, and notify affected buyers with the batch, the issue, and the remedy. The recall that moves in hours is contained; the recall that moves in days is a scandal, and the difference is the preparation.\n\nFinally, run the post-incident review and fix the cause in the process: intake, storage, listing, or supplier. The provider who treats every complaint as a system signal is the provider whose complaint rate falls, and the falling complaint rate is what the marketplace ranking, and the accreditation, both reward.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Insurance and Liability Basics for Providers',
                "summary" => "Know what a claim could cost you.",
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Every provider carries liability, and the provider who understands the basics knows what a claim could cost and how to reduce the exposure. Insurance is the backstop, but the first line of defence is the practice.\n\nStart with the exposures. A product provider can be liable for a mislabelled or unsafe product; a service provider can be liable for damage done in the field, from a chemical spill to a fallen branch. The exposure is real, and the question is not whether it exists but whether you have reduced it and covered it.\n\nReduce the exposure with the practices you already know: labelled products at the labelled rate, PPE by task, the written service method, and the job records. The provider whose records show the correct practice is the provider whose claim is defensible, and the record is the cheapest liability cover available.\n\nNext, understand the insurance options: product liability for providers who sell inputs, public liability for providers who work on farms, and the coverage limits each carries. Read what is excluded, because the exclusion is where the claim is denied. A policy that does not cover your actual work is a policy that does not cover you.\n\nFinally, keep the documents current: the policy, the registrations, and the records that prove the practice. The provider who can produce the policy and the practice record in the same hour is the provider who survives a claim, and the provider who survives claims stays accredited, trusted, and in business.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    19 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Community Engagement and Grower Trust',
                'summary' => 'Earn the trust the platform depends on.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The certified field agent is the cooperative's face, and the trust the platform depends on is earned face to face, one farm at a time. Community engagement is the discipline of building that trust deliberately, because trust is the platform's real currency.\n\nStart with visibility. Attend the community meetings, the orientation sessions, and the market days, and let farmers know who you are and what the platform does for them. The agent who is seen before they are needed is the agent whose visit is expected, and an expected visit is a cooperative one.\n\nNext, be useful before you are official. Answer a question about a program, explain a document requirement, or help a grower read a notification. The farmer whose first interaction with the platform is helpful is the farmer who registers, verifies, and participates, and the agent who helps is the reason the farmer trusts the platform.\n\nThen keep every promise. If you say you will return with an answer, return. If you say the document will be checked, make sure it is. Trust is built by kept promises and broken by broken ones, and the agent who keeps promises is the agent whose verifications are met with open gates rather than suspicion.\n\nFinally, report the community's reality up the chain: the program gaps, the confusion, the needs. The agent who speaks for the farmers, with facts, is the agent the cooperative values, and the cooperative that hears its farmers is the cooperative that serves them. Trust is the loop: earned in the field, returned by the platform.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Data Privacy in the Field',
                'summary' => 'Handle grower data like your own.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Field agents collect the platform's most sensitive data: names, IDs, phone numbers, GPS coordinates, and farm records. Handling that data responsibly is not just policy; it is the trust the whole verification system rests on.\n\nStart with the collection. Collect only what the visit requires, and collect it through the platform's capture flow, which timestamps, links, and secures the record. A photo of an ID in a personal gallery is a privacy breach waiting to happen, and the platform's record is the only record the verification team should ever see.\n\nNext, protect the device. Lock the phone, keep the app updated, and never store grower data in personal notes, messages, or social media. The agent whose phone holds the registry's data in an unsecured gallery is the agent who can leak an entire LGA's personal data in one lost phone.\n\nThen share on a need-to-know basis. Grower details go to the verification team through the platform, not to colleagues' chat groups, not to buyers, not to other growers. The data shared beyond the need is the data that cannot be unshared, and the agent who respects the boundary is the agent farmers trust with their details.\n\nFinally, handle requests properly. A grower asking for their own record, or to correct it, should be routed through the support desk, not answered from memory. The agent who routes data requests correctly is the agent who keeps the platform's data clean, and clean data is the platform's promise to every grower.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    21 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Store Analytics and Conversion Optimization',
                'summary' => 'Read the funnel and fix the leaks.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Every store has a funnel: views become inquiries, inquiries become orders, and orders become repeat buyers. The seller who reads the funnel finds the leak and fixes it, and the seller who does not watches the traffic pass by.\n\nStart with the funnel numbers: views, inquiries, conversion from view to inquiry, conversion from inquiry to order, and the repeat rate. Each step has a cause: a listing with many views and few inquiries has a title, price, or photo problem; a listing with inquiries and no orders has a trust, grade, or price problem. The numbers name the problem before you guess at it.\n\nNext, fix the listing at the weak step. Rewrite the title with the words buyers search, add the honest photo that shows the real product, and price against the market with your cost known. The listing that answers the buyer's questions before they are asked converts the browser into the inquirer.\n\nThen fix the inquiry response. Reply within the day, answer the specific questions, and confirm the terms in writing. The seller who replies fast and completely converts the inquirer into the buyer, and the message history is the evidence that the conversion was honest.\n\nFinally, review the funnel monthly and fix one leak at a time. The seller who optimizes the funnel step by step watches the same traffic produce more orders, and more orders from the same traffic is the cheapest growth the marketplace offers.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Building a Seller Brand',
                'summary' => 'Become the store buyers search for by name.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "A store becomes a brand when buyers search for it by name, and the brand is built by repetition of a promise kept. The seller who builds a brand earns more than the seller who lists, because the brand is the trust that survives a price difference.\n\nStart with the promise. What can a buyer always expect from your store: the grade, the quantity, the lead time, the packing standard? Write the promise down, and keep it on every order. The store that promises clean, graded, fast-delivered produce and keeps that promise becomes the store buyers return to.\n\nNext, make the identity visible. A consistent profile image, a clear store description, and a tidy, precise catalog signal professionalism. Buyers judge a store in seconds, and the first impression decides whether they browse or leave. The small effort of a complete, consistent identity pays for itself in trust.\n\nThen let the reviews build the brand. Ask satisfied buyers for feedback, respond to criticism professionally, and fix the causes behind the complaints. The rating is the brand's public face, and the seller who tends the rating tends the brand. A strong rating is the marketing that money cannot buy.\n\nFinally, grow the brand with the business. New grades, new forms of the same product, and new buyer segments should all carry the same promise. The seller who treats the store as a brand, rather than a shelf, builds an asset that appreciates with every satisfied buyer, and that asset is the store's real value.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    134 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Data-Driven Coverage Expansion',
                'summary' => 'Expand coverage where the data points.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Coverage does not expand by ambition; it expands by evidence. The coordinator who expands coverage where the data points fills the real gaps while the one who expands by guesswork spreads the field force thin and covers nothing well.\n\nStart with the gap map. Count verified growers and active farms per LGA, divide by the estimated totals, and rank the LGAs by coverage percentage. The gap map shows where the unregistered, unverified growers actually are, and the lowest percentages are the expansion priorities, not the loudest requests.\n\nNext, estimate the demand. An LGA with many unverified growers, active program interest, and provider presence is an LGA where a field agent's work compounds. An LGA with few growers and no providers is an LGA where the same agent's work spreads thin. The data that estimates demand is the data that prioritises the investment.\n\nThen plan the expansion in steps: assign the agent, set the target, schedule the visits, and review the evidence quality after the first month. The expansion that is measured from day one is the expansion that works by quarter three, and the unmeasured expansion is the one that quietly fails.\n\nFinally, re-map after each quarter. Coverage changes as the registry grows, and the gap map of last quarter is the history, not the plan. The coordinator who expands coverage on the data, in steps, with a review, turns the state's coverage map from an ambition into a fact.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Training and Certifying Field Agents',
                'summary' => 'Build the field force the state depends on.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The state's coverage is only as good as the agents who execute it, and the coordinator who trains and certifies agents deliberately builds a field force that verifies with defensible evidence. Training is a program, not a conversation.\n\nStart with the curriculum: the verification standards, the GPS and photo evidence requirements, the fraud red flags, and the reporting flow. The agent who knows the standards verifies to them, and the agent who guesses verifies to their own habit. The curriculum is the difference between the two.\n\nNext, pair every new agent with a senior agent for their first visits. The supervised visit is where the standards become skills: the GPS fix taken properly, the landmark photo that ties the coordinate to the place, the factual note that a reviewer can act on. The agent who learns on supervised visits is the agent whose first solo verifications pass review.\n\nThen certify against the standard. The agent who completes the training, passes the assessment, and demonstrates the evidence standard in supervised visits is the agent who verifies unsupervised. The certification is the gate, and the gate is what keeps the verification quality the state's reputation depends on.\n\nFinally, keep training current. New fraud patterns, new app flows, and new program rules all change the field work, and the coordinator who refreshes the training is the coordinator whose field force stays sharp. The sharp field force is the state's coverage, and coverage is the platform's promise to every grower.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    149 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Seedling Grading and Pricing',
                'summary' => 'Grade and price so the nursery profits.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Nursery profit lives in the grading and the pricing, and the operator who grades honestly and prices from cost earns a living while the operator who grades by hope and prices by guess works harder for less. The two disciplines go together.\n\nStart with the grading criteria: height, stem girth, leaf count, root health, and freedom from disease. Grade at transplanting readiness, and grade into clear classes: grade one for the premium buyer, grade two for interplanting, and culls that are never sold as planting stock. The nursery that sells culls as grade one sells its reputation with them, and the reputation is the nursery's only premium asset.\n\nNext, calculate the true cost per seedling: nuts, media, trays, water, shade, labour, and transport, divided by the seedlings that actually made it to sale. The cost per seedling is the number the price must beat, and the operator who never calculates it prices blind. A nursery that knows its cost prices to profit; one that does not prices to survive.\n\nThen price by grade against the regional market. Check what grade one fetches in your area, and price grade two below it. The price that reflects the grade and the cost is the price that sells the nursery's best stock first, and the best stock sold first is the nursery's reputation sold forward.\n\nFinally, keep the batch records that justify the price: source palm, sowing date, germination counts, and grade counts. The buyer of certified seedlings asks for the record, and the record is what turns a good seedling into a premium sale. Grade, cost, price, record: the four numbers of a profitable nursery.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Transporting Seedlings Safely',
                'summary' => 'Deliver the nursery\'s work intact.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "A nursery's reputation arrives with the seedlings, and the seedling that arrives damaged is a sale lost and a customer lost with it. Transporting seedlings safely is a discipline of packing, timing, and handling that starts before the vehicle moves.\n\nStart with the preparation. Water the seedlings well the day before transport, because a hydrated seedling survives the journey and a dry one arrives stressed. Harden the seedlings off if they are coming from full shade, and keep the root zone intact: bare-root seedlings for short trips, potted or balled seedlings for longer ones.\n\nNext, pack to protect. Stack trays or pots so they cannot slide or crush, keep the load shaded and covered, and never stack heavy items on seedlings. The vehicle should be clean, dry, and free of chemicals, and the load should be secured against the brake and the turn. The two minutes of packing is the two minutes that decide the condition at the farm gate.\n\nThen manage the clock. Transport in the cool hours, not the midday heat, and deliver the same day wherever possible. A seedling that sits in a hot vehicle for a day is a seedling that arrives with wilted fronds and a week of setback, and the setback is visible at the next visit.\n\nFinally, hand over with care: count the seedlings against the order, note the condition, and give the buyer the planting guidance and the batch record. The nursery that delivers intact, on time, with the record is the nursery whose buyers return every planting season, and the returning buyer is the nursery's real business.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    150 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Seasonal Monitoring Calendars',
                'summary' => 'Scout on the pest\'s schedule, not yours.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Pests and diseases have seasons, and the farm that monitors on the pest's calendar catches the outbreak before the farm that monitors whenever the manager remembers. The seasonal monitoring calendar is the field officer's planning tool.\n\nStart with the history. Last season's case records show which pests and diseases arrived when, and the pattern is the best forecast the farm has. The rhinoceros beetle's surge after certain weather, the bud rot that follows wet, injured crowns: the calendar is written in the farm's own records, and the operator who reads it scouts in advance.\n\nNext, build the calendar by month: what to scout for, what the signs are, and what the first action is. Mark the critical windows: the growth flushes when pests arrive, the wet spells when disease spreads, and the harvest windows when handling quality matters most. The calendar that is written down is the calendar that is followed.\n\nThen assign the scouting. A calendar without an owner is a wish; a calendar with a named person, a route, and a frequency is a system. The scout who walks the same route weekly sees the change that a random walk misses, because the eye that knows the normal spots the abnormal.\n\nFinally, review the calendar each season against the records. What arrived early, what arrived late, and what did not arrive at all. The farm that updates its monitoring calendar from its own data scouts smarter every season, and the smart scout is the cheap control that beats the expensive spray.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Record-Based Disease Prediction',
                'summary' => 'Predict the outbreak from the pattern.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The best disease control is the one applied before the disease arrives, and the prediction comes from the farm's own records. Record-based prediction is the discipline of reading the pattern in the past to act in the present.\n\nStart with the case log: every disease sighting with its date, the block, the signs, the weather, and what was done. The log is the farm's disease history, and the history is the dataset the prediction is built on. A log kept for two seasons is a log that starts to forecast.\n\nNext, look for the correlations. Which diseases follow which weather, which blocks are always first, and which varieties show which signs? The pattern that repeats in the records is the pattern that will repeat in the field, and the operator who knows the pattern pre-positions the response: the sanitation, the spacing, the resistant variety, or the monitoring week.\n\nThen act on the prediction with the cheapest control. Cultural prevention first, the monitoring tightened, and the treatment reserved for the threshold the records justify. The prediction that triggers a measured, cheap action is the prediction that pays; the prediction that triggers a panic spray is the one that costs.\n\nFinally, close the loop: record what the prediction predicted and what happened. The prediction that is checked against the outcome is the one that improves next season, and the farm that predicts from records and checks against outcomes is the farm that manages disease instead of reacting to it.",
            ],
        ],
    ],

    151 => [
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Member Meetings and Transparent Voting',
                'summary' => 'Run meetings that members trust.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "A cooperative is governed by its members, and the meeting is where the governance happens. The meeting that is run transparently builds the member trust that the cooperative runs on, and the meeting that is run by habit builds the apathy that ends it.\n\nStart with the agenda, published before the meeting. Members should know what will be decided, and the agenda published in advance is the agenda members can prepare for. The member who can read the agenda before the meeting is the member who participates in the meeting, and participation is the cooperative's lifeblood.\n\nNext, run the meeting to the rules: quorum, motions, discussion, and a recorded vote. The vote that is recorded, with who voted and how, is the vote that cannot be questioned later, and the record is the cooperative's defence against the dispute that follows every close decision. Transparency is not the vote; it is the recorded vote.\n\nThen publish the minutes and the decisions, with the numbers: the accounts, the membership, the programs. The member who can read the decision and the numbers is the member who trusts the leadership, and the trust is what makes the next program, the next levy, and the next season work.\n\nFinally, hold the leadership to the calendar: the annual general meeting, the elections, and the audits, on time, every time. The cooperative that keeps its calendar keeps its members, and the members who keep their meetings keep their cooperative.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Financial Transparency and Member Audits',
                'summary' => 'Open the books and let the members see.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Money is where member trust is won or lost, and the cooperative that opens its books and lets the members audit keeps the trust that the closed books lose. Financial transparency is a practice, not a promise.\n\nStart with the published accounts: the income, the spending, the balances, and the programs' funds, published to the members on a fixed rhythm. The member who can read the accounts is the member who can ask the questions, and the question the member can ask is the question the leadership must answer. Published accounts are the cooperative's quiet auditor.\n\nNext, run the member audit: a small committee of members, not the leadership, reviewing the books and the receipts against the accounts. The member audit is the control that catches the error and the misuse alike, and the audit run by members is the audit the members believe. An audit run by the audited is not an audit.\n\nThen publish the audit's findings, including the findings that are uncomfortable. The leadership that publishes the finding with the fix is the leadership that keeps the trust, and the trust is the asset every program, levy, and election depends on. The hidden finding is the one that ends leaderships; the published one is the one that builds them.\n\nFinally, act on every finding: fix the process, retrain the treasurer, and report the fix at the next meeting. The cooperative that closes the loop on its audits is the cooperative whose members invest their money and their seasons, and the invested member is the cooperative's real capital.",
            ],
        ],
    ],
];
